# What not to upload to Vercel.
#
# This repository is a pipeline that happens to contain a static site: 18 MB
# tracked, of which the site is 2.2 MB. The rest is the 12 MB scanned PDF and
# the intermediate JSON the pipeline writes on the way to the payload, none of
# which the deployment reads -- `vercel.json` serves `app/` and runs no build.
#
# TWO THINGS ABOUT THE SYNTAX, BOTH OF WHICH HAVE ALREADY BITTEN:
#
# 1. Every pattern is anchored with a leading slash, and must be. These are
#    gitignore patterns: an unanchored `data/` matches a directory of that name
#    at ANY depth, which would take `app/data/` with it -- and
#    app/data/dictionary.json is the whole dictionary. The site would deploy
#    and then fail to load, with nothing in the build log to say why.
#
# 2. A `#` only begins a comment at the START of a line. A trailing comment is
#    part of the pattern, so `/source/   # the PDF` silently matches nothing.
#    Keep every note on its own line, as below.

# the scanned PDF, 12 MB
/source/

# parse output and diagnostics. NOT app/data, which is the payload.
/data/

# the build tooling; the deployment runs none of it
/pipeline/

# source assessment, data model, roadmap, README screenshot
/docs/

# the GitHub Pages workflow
/.github/

# scratch (gitignored anyway)
/.tmp/

/README.md
/LICENSE

# kept out so nothing can mistake this for a Node project to build
/package.json
